---
import PageShell from '../../components/nav/PageShell.astro';
import { theorems, deferred } from '../../data/formalization';
import { papers } from '../../data/papers';
import { TESTS, numberWord } from '../../data/evidence';
import { LEAN_URL, THEOREM_MAP_URL, LEAN_STATUS_URL, PAPERS_URL, CRATE_URL } from '../../consts';

const title = 'Proof | DeepCausality Quantum';
const description =
  'What Lean proves, what the tests check, which claim Lean refutes, which targets have no Lean proof, and which papers the crate implements. Each row names the file that shows it.';

const cap = (s: string) => s.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + s.slice(1);
const split = (s: string) => {
  const [file, item] = s.split(' :: ');
  return { file, item };
};

const cited = papers.filter((p) => p.citedFrom.length > 0);
const used = papers.filter((p) => p.citedFrom.length === 0 && (p.usedBy ?? []).length > 0);
---
<PageShell title={title} description={description} path="/proof/">
  <header class="doc-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Proof</p>
    <h1>{cap(numberWord(TESTS.proved))} theorems, proved in Lean and checked in Rust.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Each theorem has a Lean proof and a Rust test that checks the same statement on numbers.
      Lean also refutes one claim: commuting operators can stop commuting under a partial trace.
      {cap(numberWord(TESTS.deferred))} targets have tests and no Lean proof. Every test in the
      crate passes.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="theorems">
    <h2>Ten of the theorems concern the partial trace.</h2>
    <p>
      They are six laws of the partial trace, the boundary theorem, the commutator identity and the
      two statements of the counterexample below. Two more make the Choi action linear, and two show
      that exact abstraction links compose.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pinned Mathlib has no partial trace and no Choi–Jamiołkowski layer, so the proofs build
      both from first principles under
      <a href={LEAN_URL}><code>lean/DeepCausalityFormal/Quantum/</code></a>. Each row pairs the
      Lean declaration with the Rust test that checks the same statement on numbers. The rows come
      from the quantum section of <a href={THEOREM_MAP_URL}><code>lean/THEOREM_MAP.md</code></a>.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="table-stack">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Statement</th><th>Lean declaration</th><th>Rust witness</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {theorems.map((t) => (
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Statement"><span class="num">{t.statement}</span><br /><code class="tid">{t.id}</code></td>
              <td data-label="Lean declaration"><code class="item">{split(t.lean).item}</code><br /><span class="fpath">{split(t.lean).file}</span></td>
              <td data-label="Rust witness"><code class="item">{split(t.witness).item}</code><br /><span class="fpath">{split(t.witness).file}</span></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="refuted">
    <h2>A partial trace can break commutation.</h2>
    <p>
      Two operators that commute on a whole system can have traced parts that do not. Take
      operators X and Y on a system with parts A and B, with <code>[X, Y] = 0</code>. Lean proves an
      explicit pair for which <code>[Tr_B X, Tr_B Y] = [[0, 4], [−4, 0]]</code>, which equals
      <code>+4i·σy</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two related statements are true. The boundary version holds: if the boundary operator
      commutes with M, its kept-side part commutes with <code>Tr_B M</code>. The commutator
      identity, <code>Tr_B([Z⊗1, M]) = [Z, Tr_B M]</code>, holds with no hypothesis.
    </p>
    <p>
      The identity lets the crate turn a measured residual into a bound. A residual ε on
      <code>[Z⊗1, M]</code> bounds <code>[Z, Tr_B M]</code> by <code>√(d_B)·ε</code> in Frobenius
      norm, where <code>d_B</code> is the dimension traced away. A commutator within tolerance
      therefore certifies the traced conclusion at <code>√(d_B)</code> times that tolerance and
      never the exact one, and the check returns the bound with a flag against the caller’s
      tolerance.
    </p>
    <p>
      The boundary theorem assumes exact equality over a commutative ring, which floating-point
      arithmetic cannot supply. The bound covers that gap.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="tests">
    <h2>Every test in the crate passes.</h2>
    <p>
      The suite runs under <code>cargo test -p deep_causality_quantum --all-features</code>. It
      includes cases where a check must reject its input and cases where a check has nothing to
      examine, such as an empty fault set.
    </p>
    <p class="next-links">
      <a href={`${CRATE_URL}/tests`}>The test tree ↗</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="deferred">
    <h2>{cap(numberWord(TESTS.deferred))} targets have tests and no Lean proof.</h2>
    <p>
      They are the Choi–Jamiołkowski reconstruction isomorphism and six theorems about quantum
      causal models. The theorem map and <a href={LEAN_STATUS_URL}><code>LEAN_QUANTUM.md</code></a>
      list them, and the crate carries numerical or property-test witnesses for each.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>LEAN_QUANTUM.md</code> says these proofs need net-new Mathlib machinery. For
      <code>quantum.unitary_factorization</code> it gives the machinery: direct-sum and C*-algebra
      theory that Mathlib lacks.
    </p>
    <dl class="deflist">
      {deferred.map((d) => (
        <div>
          <dt><code>{d.id}</code></dt>
          <dd>
            <p class="dstate">{d.statement}</p>
            <p class="dtoday">{d.today}</p>
          </dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>
    <p>
      <code>LEAN_QUANTUM.md</code> lists the Markov freeze check, the naturality check, the
      structural precheck, the fault propagator and the decoder validation as witnessed by tests;
      none claims a theorem. The <a href="/checks/">checks page</a> marks each check.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="papers">
    <h2>The implementation is built on published science.</h2>
    <p>
      The crate implements published research, and every work below is cited in the code that
      uses it. {cap(numberWord(cited.length))} shape the crate’s modules, and the first list gives
      what the crate takes from each. {cap(numberWord(used.length))} more stand behind the sensing
      examples and their verifications. The PDFs the repository holds are under
      <a href={PAPERS_URL}><code>papers/</code></a>.
    </p>
    <h3>Cited from the crate’s modules</h3>
    <ol class="papers">
      {cited.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <p class="ptitle">{p.title}</p>
          <p class="pmeta">{p.authors}. {p.ref}, {p.year}.</p>
          {p.usedFor && <p class="pused">{p.usedFor}</p>}
          <p class="pcite">Cited from <code>{p.citedFrom.slice(0, 4).join(', ')}</code>{p.citedFrom.length > 4 ? ` and ${p.citedFrom.length - 4} more` : ''}.</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <h3 id="sensing-papers">Cited by the sensing examples and their verifications</h3>
    <ol class="papers">
      {used.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <p class="ptitle">{p.title}</p>
          <p class="pmeta">{p.authors}. {p.ref}, {p.year}.</p>
          {p.usedFor && <p class="pused">{p.usedFor}</p>}
          <p class="pcite">Cited by <code>{(p.usedBy ?? []).join(', ')}</code>.</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

  </section>
</PageShell>

<style>
  .tid { color: var(--fg-2); background: none; padding: 0; font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); }
  .table-scroll table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; }
  .table-scroll th, .table-scroll td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; padding: var(--space-3); border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1); }
  .table-scroll thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase; font-weight: 400; color: var(--fg-2); border-top: none;
  }
  .table-scroll td code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    .table-scroll table { table-layout: fixed; }
    .table-scroll th:nth-child(1) { width: 29%; }
    .table-scroll th:nth-child(2) { width: 31%; }
    .table-scroll th:nth-child(3) { width: 40%; }
  }
  .item { background: none; padding: 0; color: var(--fg-0); font-size: var(--t-mono-sm); }
  .fpath { color: var(--fg-2); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  .dstate { margin: 0 0 var(--space-1); color: var(--fg-0); }
  .dtoday { margin: 0; color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }

  .papers { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 var(--space-5); padding: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1); max-width: var(--measure); }
  .papers li { padding: var(--space-4) 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-1); }
  .papers p { margin: 0 0 var(--space-1); }
  .ptitle { color: var(--fg-0); font-weight: var(--fw-heading); }
  .pmeta { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }
  .pused { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }
  .pcite { color: var(--fg-2); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }
  .pcite code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
</style>
